[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1: The landscape of Geneverse. To generate LLMs for genomic and proteomic analysis, we incorporate the training datasets from rephrased descriptions for gene functions as well as synthetic descriptions from GPT 3.5. We then adjust the base model with different strategies and select the best candidate. To generate MLLMs for genomic and proteomic analysis, we incorporate the training datasets from known databases, including both descriptions and corresponding images. We then finetune the base model with different strategies and select the best candidate. The logo of Geneverse is generated by DALLE (OpenAI, 2024).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow figure for a biomedical AI model named **Geneverse**.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left panel labeled **“Preprocessing steps”**:\n  - Input stack labeled **“Descriptions from databases”** containing repeated cards with text like **“MERTK Ensemble id... HGNC id ...”**\n  - Arrow labeled **“GPT 3.5 rephrased”** pointing toward augmented text descriptions.\n  - A **GPT-3.5** icon combined with a box labeled **“Biomedical instructions”**, feeding into generated descriptions.\n\n- Middle panel labeled **“Training datasets”**:\n  - Top subsection: **“Augmented Descriptions”**, showing blue stacked cards labeled **“MERTK Ensemble id... HGNC id ...”**\n  - Middle subsection: **“Descriptions from GPT 3.5”**, showing another set of blue stacked description cards.\n  - Bottom subsection: **“Descriptions from databases”**, showing red stacked cards with text like **“This protein/gene is known as...”**\n  - Bottom also includes **“Biomedical images”**, illustrated by a protein structure image and a microscopy/histology-like image.\n  - Text and biomedical images are visually combined with a plus symbol, indicating multimodal training inputs.\n\n- Right model panel:\n  - Dashed red box containing model/source icons, including a stylized **M**, a circular animal/cartoon-like icon, a **Google “G”**, and another icon, with ellipsis between them.\n  - This panel is labeled externally by the title/logo **“Geneverse”**.\n\n- Output/application area:\n  - Arrow from the model panel to **“Downstream applications”**.\n  - Icons suggest biological or biomedical tasks involving DNA/gene symbols, a cloud/thought bubble, molecular or cellular graphics, histology/microscopy images, and green check/red cross classification-like outputs.\n\nBiological source objects shown:\n- Gene/protein descriptions from databases.\n- Specific example text references **MERTK**, **Ensembl ID**, and **HGNC ID**.\n- Biomedical instructions.\n- Biomedical images, including a protein structure and microscopy/histology-like imagery.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Database descriptions are rephrased by **GPT-3.5**.\n- GPT-3.5 plus biomedical instructions generates additional descriptions.\n- Text descriptions and biomedical images are combined into training datasets.\n- The trained/assembled Geneverse system is used for downstream biomedical applications.\n\nNo explicit quantitative findings, experimental results, or performance metrics are visible in the image."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 2: UMAPs for the gene embeddings colored by gene functional information. Panels (a)-(c) represent the outputs of LLMs trained based on datasets from NCBI+GPT 3.5, NCBI only and GPT 3.5. We report the NMI score of each embeddings followed by their sources.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with three labeled panels: **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel contents:\n- **a:** Titled **“NCBI+GPT 3.5 (0.377)”**. Shows a 2D UMAP scatterplot with axes labeled **UMAP1** and **UMAP2**. Points are colored into multiple clusters, with a large central pink cluster and smaller peripheral clusters in cyan, dark blue, green, brown, and other colors.\n- **b:** Titled **“NCBI only (0.350)”**. Shows another 2D UMAP scatterplot with similarly colored point clusters. The central pink cluster is more compact, with several separated colored clusters around it.\n- **c:** Titled **“GPT 3.5 only (0.337)”**. Shows a third 2D UMAP scatterplot. A central pink cluster is present, with separated peripheral clusters including cyan and dark blue groups.\n\nVisible transformations and interfaces:\n- The figure visualizes high-dimensional data projected into two dimensions using **UMAP**.\n- The panels compare embeddings or classifications derived from three data/model conditions: **NCBI+GPT 3.5**, **NCBI only**, and **GPT 3.5 only**.\n- Numeric values in parentheses appear to be performance or clustering metrics, but the exact metric is not specified in the image.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The title references **NCBI**, suggesting data sourced from NCBI, but no specific organism, gene, protein, disease, sample type, or biological entity is visible in the extracted image.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- All three conditions produce broadly similar UMAP cluster structures with a dominant central pink cluster and multiple smaller separated clusters.\n- The combined **NCBI+GPT 3.5** condition has the highest displayed value, **0.377**, followed by **NCBI only** at **0.350** and **GPT 3.5 only** at **0.337**."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 3: Results of sensitivity analysis for the training of different models. (a) The relation between the number of epochs and model performance of LLMs. (b) The relation between the number of cut-off length and model performance of LLMs. (c) The relation between the number of epochs and model performance of MLLMs for the protein task. (d) The relation between the number of epochs and model performance of MLLMs for the gene task. (e) The relation between the number of cut-off length and model performance of MLLMs for the protein task. (f) The relation between the number of cut-off length and model performance of MLLMs for the gene task.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot figure with six labeled panels: **a, b, c, d, e, f**. Each panel shows line plots of **Model performance** on the y-axis, with legends for three metrics: **truthfulness** in red with star markers, **structure** in cyan with circular markers, and **average** in blue with circular markers.\n\nPanels:\n- **a**: Model performance vs **Epochs** at 1, 5, 10, 20. Structure remains near 1.0; truthfulness and average increase slightly toward high performance.\n- **b**: Model performance vs **Cut-off length** at 512, 1024, 2048. All metrics remain high and mostly flat, with structure at about 1.0 and truthfulness around 0.95.\n- **c**: Model performance vs **Epochs** at 1, 5, 10. Structure remains near 1.0, while truthfulness decreases from roughly 0.35 to 0.25 and average decreases slightly.\n- **d**: Model performance vs **Epochs** at 1, 5, 10. Structure remains near 1.0; truthfulness rises sharply at epoch 10; average also increases strongly at epoch 10.\n- **e**: Model performance vs **Cut-off length** at 512, 1024, 2048. Structure increases to near 1.0; truthfulness remains low but improves from about 0.1 to 0.3; average rises from about 0.5 to about 0.65.\n- **f**: Model performance vs **Cut-off length** at 512, 1024, 2048. All metrics start near 0 at 512 and improve substantially by 1024 and 2048, with structure reaching near 1.0 and truthfulness near 0.9 at 2048.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, molecular structures, or experimental transformations are visible. The figure appears to compare model evaluation metrics across training epochs and cut-off lengths."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 15, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 1: Definition of different evaluators or scorers. For the scorer focusing on truthfulness, we evaluate the matching level of model outputs for the description of gene properties and gene functions. For the scorer focusing on structural correctness, we evaluate the correctness of the structure of model outputs by comparing them to the limitations in the prompt. The logos of scorers are generated by DALLE (OpenAI, 2024).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSchematic workflow labeled “Scorer” showing two grader modules for evaluating gene-function outputs.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Two horizontal rows/panels.\n- Top row: “Grader for truthfulness” with a green rounded input box labeled “The function of gene...” pointing by arrow to outcomes:\n  - “Correct gene names and functions – high score”\n  - “Wrong gene names and functions – low score”\n- Bottom row: “Grader for structural correctness” with the same green input box pointing to outcomes:\n  - “Correct output format – high score”\n  - “Wrong output format – low score”\n- Decorative icons appear between input and output: an owl-like figure for truthfulness and a worker/robot-like figure for structural correctness.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No specific genes, organisms, proteins, cells, tissues, datasets, or biological images are shown.\n- Biological content is limited to the text concept of “gene names and functions.”\n\nModel/interface content:\n- Depicts grader/scorer interfaces assessing generated gene-function text.\n- Separates semantic truthfulness from output-format correctness.\n\nFinding/message:\n- Correct gene-function content receives a high truthfulness score; incorrect gene-function content receives a low score.\n- Correct formatting receives a high structural score; wrong formatting receives a low score."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 15, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 2: Figures of GOEA results. Each figure represents top 10 pathways in one cluster, and the pathways are ranked by -log(Adjusted P-value).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot composed of horizontal bar charts arranged in a grid. Each panel is labeled by `-log(Adjusted P-value)` with numbered panels from approximately `0` through `9`.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panels show ranked biological process terms on the y-axis.\n- The x-axis in each panel represents `-log(Adjusted P-value)`.\n- Bars are teal/blue and indicate enrichment significance.\n- Terms appear to be Gene Ontology biological processes or similar pathway/enrichment annotations.\n\nVisible biological/process labels include examples such as:\n- Regulation of transcription elongation by RNA polymerase II\n- Positive regulation of transcription elongation by RNA polymerase II\n- Protein phosphorylation\n- Phosphorylation\n- Positive regulation of DNA-templated transcription\n- Carboxylic acid catabolic process\n- Glycolytic process\n- Fatty acid beta-oxidation process\n- Protein dephosphorylation\n- Phosphatidylinositol phosphate biosynthetic process\n- Protein glycosylation\n- tRNA methylation\n- Mitochondrial ATP synthesis coupled electron transport\n- Energy derivation by oxidation of organic compounds\n- Purine nucleoside bisphosphate metabolic process\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The figure appears to summarize enriched biological processes associated with gene sets or clusters, but the specific organism, tissue, cell type, dataset, or experimental source is not visible in the image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, computational pipeline, microscopy transformation, or interface diagram is visible.\n- The visible transformation is statistical enrichment summarized as `-log(Adjusted P-value)` bar plots.\n\nMain finding shown:\n- Different panels/clusters are enriched for distinct biological processes, including transcriptional regulation, phosphorylation/dephosphorylation, carbohydrate metabolism, fatty acid metabolism, glycosylation, mitochondrial respiration, and nucleotide metabolism."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel schematic labeled **a** and **b**, each titled **“Instruction example”** and showing an LLM-style question-answer interface.\n\nPanel **a** shows an example involving a protein structure. The prompt asks: “What is the name of this protein? Please summarize your answer in one sentence.” A small ribbon-like protein structure image appears beside the prompt. The LLM response box answers: “It is AF-O43280-F1.”\n\nPanel **b** shows an example involving biological microscopy/histology imagery. The prompt asks whether **gene APOC1** is a marker gene of **cell type Macrophage**, followed by “Please summarize your answer in one sentence.” A purple-stained tissue microscopy image appears beside the prompt. The LLM response box answers: “Yes.”\n\nBoth panels depict instruction-following model interfaces with biological source objects as inputs: a protein structure in panel **a** and a histological tissue image plus gene/cell-type query in panel **b**. The figure illustrates LLM question answering over biological or biomedical visual/contextual inputs."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a simple blue icon showing three circular user/avatar symbols connected by lines, suggesting a network or group relationship. It contains no scientific figure panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a simple peach-colored line icon of a robot with antenna, square body, arms, and a rounded base with small dots. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a simple stylized robot icon/illustration. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 6: The results of data ablation for the training process.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel grouped bar chart comparing model performance across three training dataset source conditions: `NCBI`, `GPT 3.5`, and `NCBI+GPT 3.5`.\n\nVisible labels:\n- Y-axis: `Model performance`\n- X-axis: `Sources of training datasets`\n- Legend: `truthfulness`, `structure`, `average`\n\nVisual content:\n- Each category has three bars:\n  - Coral/red: truthfulness\n  - Cyan: structure\n  - Blue-gray: average\n- Approximate values:\n  - NCBI: truthfulness ~0.95, structure 1.0, average ~0.98\n  - GPT 3.5: truthfulness ~0.80, structure 1.0, average ~0.90\n  - NCBI+GPT 3.5: all metrics 1.0\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure references NCBI as a data source but does not show biological specimens, molecules, cells, tissues, organisms, or sequences.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, workflow, transformation pipeline, or interface is shown.\n- The chart summarizes evaluation metrics for models trained on different dataset sources.\n\nFinding:\n- The combined `NCBI+GPT 3.5` training source achieves perfect displayed performance across truthfulness, structure, and average.\n- `GPT 3.5` alone has lower truthfulness and average performance than NCBI or the combined source, while structure remains at 1.0 for all sources."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 7: Relation between top-k candidates and model performance.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nLine chart titled **“topk vs. Performance (LLM-RAG)”**.\n\nVisible content:\n- Single panel plot.\n- X-axis: `topk` values `1`, `2`, `5`.\n- Y-axis: performance scale from approximately `0` to `0.9`.\n- Three plotted series with circular markers:\n  - **truthfulness** in blue\n  - **structure** in orange\n  - **average** in gray\n- Legend centered below the plot.\n\nApproximate values:\n- At `topk = 1`:\n  - truthfulness ≈ `0.65`\n  - structure ≈ `0.80`\n  - average ≈ `0.73`\n- At `topk = 2`:\n  - truthfulness ≈ `0.65`\n  - structure ≈ `0.60`\n  - average ≈ `0.63`\n- At `topk = 5`:\n  - truthfulness ≈ `0.32`\n  - structure ≈ `0.00`\n  - average ≈ `0.16`\n\nFindings shown:\n- Performance generally decreases as `topk` increases.\n- `structure` drops most sharply, reaching zero at `topk = 5`.\n- `truthfulness` is stable from `topk = 1` to `2`, then decreases at `5`.\n- The average score declines across increasing `topk`.\n\nNo biological source objects, image transformations, microscopy, molecular structures, or model architecture/interface diagrams are visible."]}]

