./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/provisional_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:6730:      "caption": "Figure 6: Workflow for extracting biological embeddings from LLM-BFT. a : LLM-BFT generates profile texts for entities of interest (e.g., a specific gene). The gene profile text is encoded by OpenAI TEXT-EMBEDDING-ADA-002 (the same text encoder GenePT uses) to obtain gene embeddings, so that GenePT (GPT-4o + ada-002) and LLM-BFT (BFT-70B + ada-002) differ only in the LLM that produces the profile text. b : For a single-cell dataset, gene embeddings are weighted by gene expression values to generate cell embeddings.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/provisional_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:7101:      "caption": "Figure 4: PROCYON models domains, peptides, and small molecules beyond proteins. a) PROCYON predicts the small molecule binding domain on proteins, where the input is the description and molecular structure of a drug, and the output is a ranked list of domains on a target protein. In this example, PROCYON retrieves domains of MGAM given the drug miglitol and correctly prioritizes the binding domain for miglitol as the first among the MGAM's nine domains. Each domain is identified by the Pfam ID and the index of the first amino acid in the domain. b) PROCYON ranks the correct drug-binding domain (highlighted in orange) on respective target proteins highly. In Q06187, the green domain ( PF00017 281 ) denotes the PROCYON-predicted binding domain, but the orange domain ( PF07714 402 ) is the correct binding domain. PROCYON predicts the binding domain as top-ranked in 24 examples, second-ranked in 5 examples, and so on. c) PROCYON can be finetuned to predict protein-peptide binding. Left, PROCYON is first finetuned on naturally occurring protein-peptide complexes from the Protein Data Bank (PDB), then tested on an experimental dataset of synthetic peptides screened for binding to the ACE2 protein. Right, the predicted binding scores for binders versus non-binders. Two-sided Mann-Whitney U test. d) Leveraging PROCYON to perform indication-specific drug target retrieval. In this example, the input consists of a novel task definition, the description of indications (nicotine addiction or major depressive disorder), and the name and structure of bupropion. PROCYON ranks known targets (norepinephrine transporter (NET), dopamine transporter (DAT), and nicotinic acetylcholinergic receptor (AChR)) of bupropion among the top 40/18,174 human proteins. Shown are 95% confidence intervals derived by perturbing words in the input prompt. ** p -value < 0 . 001 ; two-sided Wilcoxon signed rank test.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/provisional_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:8390:    "rationale": "Visible evidence supports the current no-figure status. The listed actual input route is an evaluation-time biomedical causal reasoning text prompt, but no candidate figure readably depicts that prompt or its immediate model-facing interface. The closest visible prompt-like diagram is the gene-description workflow in Figure 6, which does not match the causal-reasoning route.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/provisional_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:8433:          "Figure 6 shows a generic prompt ('Tell me about gene.') for gene-profile generation, which is a different input route.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/provisional_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:8441:        "rationale": "Visible evidence supports the current no-figure status. The listed actual input route is an evaluation-time biomedical causal reasoning text prompt, but no candidate figure readably depicts that prompt or its immediate model-facing interface. The closest visible prompt-like diagram is the gene-description workflow in Figure 6, which does not match the causal-reasoning route.",
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:116:3184,HypoGeneAgent: A Hypothesis Language Agent for Gene-Set Cluster Resolution Selection Using Perturb-seq Datasets,hypogeneagent a hypothesis language agent for geneset cluster resolution selection using perturbseq datasets,,,,2509.09740v1,"Large-scale single-cell and Perturb-seq investigations routinely involve clustering cells and subsequently annotating each cluster with Gene-Ontology (GO) terms to elucidate the underlying biological programs. However, both stages, resolution selection and functional annotation, are inherently subjective, relying on heuristics and expert curation. We present HYPOGENEAGENT, a large language model (LLM)-driven framework, transforming cluster annotation into a quantitatively optimizable task. Initially, an LLM functioning as a gene-set analyst analyzes the content of each gene program or perturbation module and generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses, accompanied by calibrated confidence scores. Subsequently, we embed every predicted description with a sentence-embedding model, compute pair-wise cosine similarities, and let the agent referee panel score (i) the internal consistency of the predictions, high average similarity within the same cluster, termed intra-cluster agreement (ii) their external distinctiveness, low similarity between clusters, termed inter-cluster separation. These two quantities are combined to produce an agent-derived resolution score, which is maximized when clusters exhibit simultaneous coherence and mutual exclusivity. When applied to a public K562 CRISPRi Perturb-seq dataset as a preliminary test, our Resolution Score selects clustering granularities that exhibit alignment with known pathway compared to classical metrics such silhouette score, modularity score for gene functional enrichment summary. These findings establish LLM agents as objective adjudicators of cluster resolution and functional annotation, thereby paving the way for fully automated, context-aware interpretation pipelines in single-cell multi-omics studies.",Ying Yuan; Xing-Yue Monica Ge; Aaron Archer Waterman; Tommaso Biancalani; David Richmond; Yogesh Pandit; Avtar Singh; Russell Littman; Jin Liu; Jan-Christian Huetter; Vladimir Ermakov,2025.0,,2025-09-10,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,,114,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""LLM ... generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses ... embed every predicted description with a sentence-embedding model"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""LLM functioning as a gene-set analyst analyzes gene programs and generates textual GO hypotheses that are used to compute intra‑cluster agreement and inter‑cluster separation"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces HYPOGENEAGENT, a new LLM‑driven framework that creates natural‑language GO hypotheses from Perturb‑seq data and uses these texts to evaluate clustering, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and a substantive text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,LLM ... generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses ... embed every predicted description with a sentence-embedding model,LLM functioning as a gene-set analyst analyzes gene programs and generates textual GO hypotheses that are used to compute intra‑cluster agreement and inter‑cluster separation,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces HYPOGENEAGENT, a new LLM‑driven framework that creates natural‑language GO hypotheses from Perturb‑seq data and uses these texts to evaluate clustering, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes using an existing LLM to generate GO hypotheses, making it a wrapper around a pre‑trained model rather than a new primary generative model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes using an existing LLM to generate GO hypotheses, making it a wrapper around a pre‑trained model rather than a new primary generative model.",PASS,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""LLM ... analyzes the content of each gene program ... generates ... hypotheses"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes using an existing LLM to generate textual GO hypotheses from gene‑set data and then scoring clusters; no new multimodal generative model is introduced, so it is an application‑wrapper.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses,LLM ... analyzes the content of each gene program ... generates ... hypotheses,generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes using an existing LLM to generate textual GO hypotheses from gene‑set data and then scoring clusters; no new multimodal generative model is introduced, so it is an application‑wrapper.",PASS|EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,application_wrapper,,generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses,LLM ... analyzes the content of each gene program ... generates ... hypotheses,generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes using an existing LLM to generate textual GO hypotheses from gene‑set data and then scoring clusters; no new multimodal generative model is introduced, so it is an application‑wrapper.",EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:124:3192,Modeling Gene Expression Distributional Shifts for Unseen Genetic Perturbations,modeling gene expression distributional shifts for unseen genetic perturbations,,,,2507.02980v1,"We train a neural network to predict distributional responses in gene expression following genetic perturbations. This is an essential task in early-stage drug discovery, where such responses can offer insights into gene function and inform target identification. Existing methods only predict changes in the mean expression, overlooking stochasticity inherent in single-cell data. In contrast, we offer a more realistic view of cellular responses by modeling expression distributions. Our model predicts gene-level histograms conditioned on perturbations and outperforms baselines in capturing higher-order statistics, such as variance, skewness, and kurtosis, at a fraction of the training cost. To generalize to unseen perturbations, we incorporate prior knowledge via gene embeddings from large language models (LLMs). While modeling a richer output space, the method remains competitive in predicting mean expression changes. This work offers a practical step towards more expressive and biologically informative models of perturbation effects.",Kalyan Ramakrishnan; Jonathan G. Hedley; Sisi Qu; Puneet K. Dokania; Philip H. S. Torr; Cesar A. Prada-Medina; Julien Fauqueur; Kaspar Martens,2025.0,,2025-07-01,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,,122,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a new neural network that predicts gene‑expression distribution shifts and uses gene embeddings from LLMs, but it does not mention natural‑language text, prompts, or generation, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract describes a new neural network that predicts gene‑expression distribution shifts and uses gene embeddings from LLMs, but it does not mention natural‑language text, prompts, or generation, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a neural network that predicts gene‑level histograms (distributional responses) without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism, so it is not a generative model. It incorporates gene embeddings from large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract proposes a neural network that predicts gene‑level histograms (distributional responses) without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism, so it is not a generative model. It incorporates gene embeddings from large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract describes a new neural network that predicts gene‑expression distribution shifts and uses gene embeddings from LLMs, but it does not mention natural‑language text, prompts, or generation, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident. | The abstract proposes a neural network that predicts gene‑level histograms (distributional responses) without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism, so it is not a generative model. It incorporates gene embeddings from large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:146:3214,Geneverse: A collection of Open-source Multimodal Large Language Models for Genomic and Proteomic Research,geneverse a collection of opensource multimodal large language models for genomic and proteomic research,,,,2406.15534v1,"The applications of large language models (LLMs) are promising for biomedical and healthcare research. Despite the availability of open-source LLMs trained using a wide range of biomedical data, current research on the applications of LLMs to genomics and proteomics is still limited. To fill this gap, we propose a collection of finetuned LLMs and multimodal LLMs (MLLMs), known as Geneverse, for three novel tasks in genomic and proteomic research. The models in Geneverse are trained and evaluated based on domain-specific datasets, and we use advanced parameter-efficient finetuning techniques to achieve the model adaptation for tasks including the generation of descriptions for gene functions, protein function inference from its structure, and marker gene selection from spatial transcriptomic data. We demonstrate that adapted LLMs and MLLMs perform well for these tasks and may outperform closed-source large-scale models based on our evaluations focusing on both truthfulness and structural correctness. All of the training strategies and base models we used are freely accessible.",Tianyu Liu; Yijia Xiao; Xiao Luo; Hua Xu; W. Jim Zheng; Hongyu Zhao,2024.0,,2024-06-21,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,,144,"{""paper_type"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Geneverse, a collection of finetuned multimodal LLMs that generate natural-language descriptions from genomic and proteomic data, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge.""}",benchmark_resource,yes,yes,yes,benchmark_resource,none,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents Geneverse, a collection of finetuned multimodal LLMs that generate natural-language descriptions from genomic and proteomic data, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces finetuned LLMs that generate gene function descriptions, indicating a generative decoder architecture, and cites use of open‑source LLMs pretrained on broad biomedical data, fitting a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,generation of descriptions for gene functions,clear_include,"The abstract introduces finetuned LLMs that generate gene function descriptions, indicating a generative decoder architecture, and cites use of open‑source LLMs pretrained on broad biomedical data, fitting a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.",EXCLUDE,PASS,True,"{""paper_type"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces Geneverse as a collection of open‑source finetuned multimodal LLMs, which is a resource rather than a single primary model; thus it is classified as a benchmark/resource and excluded. The abstract clearly mentions generation of textual gene/protein descriptions, confirming text component, text‑bio bridge, and generative model presence.""}",benchmark_resource,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,benchmark_resource,none,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions,none,"The abstract introduces Geneverse as a collection of open‑source finetuned multimodal LLMs, which is a resource rather than a single primary model; thus it is classified as a benchmark/resource and excluded. The abstract clearly mentions generation of textual gene/protein descriptions, confirming text component, text‑bio bridge, and generative model presence.",EXCLUDE|PASS,benchmark_resource,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,benchmark_resource,,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions,none,"The abstract introduces Geneverse as a collection of open‑source finetuned multimodal LLMs, which is a resource rather than a single primary model; thus it is classified as a benchmark/resource and excluded. The abstract clearly mentions generation of textual gene/protein descriptions, confirming text component, text‑bio bridge, and generative model presence.",EXCLUDE,benchmark_resource,,EXCLUDE,benchmark_resource,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:150:3218,MuSe-GNN: Learning Unified Gene Representation From Multimodal Biological Graph Data,musegnn learning unified gene representation from multimodal biological graph data,,,,2310.02275v1,"Discovering genes with similar functions across diverse biomedical contexts poses a significant challenge in gene representation learning due to data heterogeneity. In this study, we resolve this problem by introducing a novel model called Multimodal Similarity Learning Graph Neural Network, which combines Multimodal Machine Learning and Deep Graph Neural Networks to learn gene representations from single-cell sequencing and spatial transcriptomic data. Leveraging 82 training datasets from 10 tissues, three sequencing techniques, and three species, we create informative graph structures for model training and gene representations generation, while incorporating regularization with weighted similarity learning and contrastive learning to learn cross-data gene-gene relationships. This novel design ensures that we can offer gene representations containing functional similarity across different contexts in a joint space. Comprehensive benchmarking analysis shows our model's capacity to effectively capture gene function similarity across multiple modalities, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in gene representation learning by up to 97.5%. Moreover, we employ bioinformatics tools in conjunction with gene representations to uncover pathway enrichment, regulation causal networks, and functions of disease-associated or dosage-sensitive genes. Therefore, our model efficiently produces unified gene representations for the analysis of gene functions, tissue functions, diseases, and species evolution.",Tianyu Liu; Yuge Wang; Rex Ying; Hongyu Zhao,2023.0,,2023-09-29,,['arxiv'],1,[],1,,,,,148,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces a novel multimodal GNN model for gene representation using single‑cell and spatial transcriptomic data, with no mention of natural‑language text or text‑based conditioning, so the text component and bridge are absent.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces a novel multimodal GNN model for gene representation using single‑cell and spatial transcriptomic data, with no mention of natural‑language text or text‑based conditioning, so the text component and bridge are absent.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a graph‑neural‑network model that learns unified gene representations but contains no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism, indicating it is not generative; it does pretrain on 82 datasets across tissues, techniques, and species, showing foundation‑model‑like breadth.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a graph‑neural‑network model that learns unified gene representations but contains no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism, indicating it is not generative; it does pretrain on 82 datasets across tissues, techniques, and species, showing foundation‑model‑like breadth.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract introduces a novel multimodal GNN model for gene representation using single‑cell and spatial transcriptomic data, with no mention of natural‑language text or text‑based conditioning, so the text component and bridge are absent. | The abstract describes a graph‑neural‑network model that learns unified gene representations but contains no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism, indicating it is not generative; it does pretrain on 82 datasets across tissues, techniques, and species, showing foundation‑model‑like breadth.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:239:3276,A Multi-Modal Foundation Model Across Species for Interpreting Gene Functions,a multimodal foundation model across species for interpreting gene functions,,,,,"In this work, we develop a multi-modal foundation model,  and gene expression levels based  on single-cell transcriptomics.  HyenaDNA is a genomic language model pre-trained with",['T Liu'],,NeurIPS 2025 2nd Workshop on Multi-modal …,,https://openreview.net/forum?id=U1DWJKbNte,['google_scholar'],1,[],1,,,,,206,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a multi‑modal foundation model trained on single‑cell transcriptomics (biological modality) but provides no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, indicating no text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract describes a multi‑modal foundation model trained on single‑cell transcriptomics (biological modality) but provides no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, indicating no text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces a new multi‑modal foundation model (HyenaDNA) but lacks any mention of decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other generative mechanisms, indicating no generative architecture; it explicitly calls the model a foundation model, providing FM evidence.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_include,"The abstract introduces a new multi‑modal foundation model (HyenaDNA) but lacks any mention of decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other generative mechanisms, indicating no generative architecture; it explicitly calls the model a foundation model, providing FM evidence.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract describes a multi‑modal foundation model trained on single‑cell transcriptomics (biological modality) but provides no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, indicating no text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract introduces a new multi‑modal foundation model (HyenaDNA) but lacks any mention of decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other generative mechanisms, indicating no generative architecture; it explicitly calls the model a foundation model, providing FM evidence.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:298:3315,scLong: A billion-parameter foundation model for capturing long-range gene context in single-cell transcriptomics,sclong a billionparameter foundation model for capturing longrange gene context in singlecell transcriptomics,,,,,"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has revolutionized the study of cellular heterogeneity by providing gene expression data at single-cell resolution, uncovering insights into rare cell populations, cell-cell interactions, and gene regulation. Foundation models pretrained on large-scale scRNA-seq datasets have shown great promise in analyzing such data, but existing approaches are often limited to modeling a small subset of highly expressed genes and lack the integration of external genespecific knowledge. To address these limitations, we present sc-Long, a billion-parameter foundation model pretrained on 48 million cells. sc-Long performs self-attention across the entire set of 28,000 genes in the human genome. This enables the model to capture long-range dependencies between all genes, including lowly expressed ones, which often play critical roles in cellular processes but are typically excluded by existing foundation models. Additionally, sc-Long integrates gene knowledge from the Gene Ontology using a graph convolutional network, enriching its contextual understanding of gene functions and relationships. In extensive evaluations, sc-Long surpasses both stateof-the-art scRNA-seq foundation models and task-specific models across diverse tasks, including predicting transcriptional responses to genetic and chemical perturbations, forecasting cancer drug responses, and inferring gene regulatory networks.","['D Bai', 'S Mo', 'R Zhang', 'Y Luo', 'J Gao', 'JP Yang']",2026.0,Nature …,,https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-69102-y,['google_scholar'],1,[],1,,crossref_title,185.0,,245,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces scLong, a billion-parameter foundation model for scRNA‑seq data and integrates Gene Ontology via a graph network, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces scLong, a billion-parameter foundation model for scRNA‑seq data and integrates Gene Ontology via a graph network, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a billion‑parameter self‑attention foundation model for prediction tasks (perturbation response, drug response, GRN inference) with no indication of a generative mechanism, leading to generative_model_present=no and exclusion under EC3_not_generative.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a billion‑parameter self‑attention foundation model for prediction tasks (perturbation response, drug response, GRN inference) with no indication of a generative mechanism, leading to generative_model_present=no and exclusion under EC3_not_generative.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract introduces scLong, a billion-parameter foundation model for scRNA‑seq data and integrates Gene Ontology via a graph network, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents. | The abstract presents a billion‑parameter self‑attention foundation model for prediction tasks (perturbation response, drug response, GRN inference) with no indication of a generative mechanism, leading to generative_model_present=no and exclusion under EC3_not_generative.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:384:                TALISMAN (Terminological ArtificiaL Intelligence SuMmarization of Annotation and Narratives) uses generative AI to perform gene set function summarization as a complement to standard enrichment analysis. This method can use different sources of gene functional information: (1) structured text derived from curated ontological KB annotations, (2) ontology-free narrative gene summaries, or (3) direct retrieval from the model.
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:441:3389,Tahoe-100M: A Giga-Scale Single-Cell Perturbation Atlas for Context-Dependent Gene Function and Cellular Modeling,tahoe100m a gigascale singlecell perturbation atlas for contextdependent gene function and cellular modeling,,,,,"Abstract
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:985:3629,Abstract 1214: SPACE: Spatially resolved multiomic analysis for high-throughput CRISPR screening in 3D models.,abstract 1214 space spatially resolved multiomic analysis for highthroughput crispr screening in 3d models,10.1158/1538-7445.am2026-1214,,,,"High-content single-cell perturbation screens are pivotal for elucidating gene functions and uncovering novel biology, yet conventional methods necessitate cell dissociation, forfeiting critical spatial information essential for dissecting cell-cell interactions and tissue architecture in complex microenvironments. While spatial CRISPR screening mitigates this partially, existing technologies are constrained by hypothesis-driven phenotyping panels limited to sparse RNA or protein coverage, curtailing comprehensive gene function assessment and discovery breadth.To overcome these barriers, we developed SPACE (SPAtial Cell Exploration), a pioneering platform that fuses whole-transcriptome profiling, CRISPR perturbations, and multiplexed protein detection at single-cell resolution within intact 3D tissue contexts. SPACE delivers unbiased, transcriptome-wide readouts alongside compatibility for up to 76 protein markers, vastly expanding phenotypic landscapes in spatial screens. As the highest-plex multimodal spatial CRISPR assay to date, SPACE achieves this at unprecedented scale and affordability and largely outperforms sequencing-based alternatives in efficiency.We applied SPACE across 42 gene perturbations in cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) co-cultured with tumor cells in 3D spheroids, yielding multidimensional multiomic datasets from hundreds of spheroids. High-confidence guide RNA detection was coupled with robust endogenous mRNA characterization. Unbiased analyses uncovered new insights on CAF-tumor dynamics: extracellular matrix (ECM) remodeling, spatially resolved ligand-receptor interactions, and perturbation-specific gene variability.Notably, ISG20 knockout in CAFs profoundly suppressed multiple matrix metalloproteinases - an unreported link validated orthogonally - implicating ISG20 in novel ECM regulation and tumor progression. Some perturbations were further revealed to reshape intercellular signaling, revealing knockout-dependent spatial ligand-receptor shifts and coordinated expression signatures that underscore microenvironmental crosstalk in tumor phenotypes.Culminating in a landmark demonstration, SPACE simultaneously captured whole transcriptomes, CRISPR identities, and 68 protein markers on one slide, enabling holistic perturbation phenotyping. This transformative technology propels spatial CRISPR screening into translational realms, facilitating target and biomarker identification in multicellular models mirroring human tissue intricacy. By merging high-throughput perturbations with spatially resolved multiomics at transcriptome scale, SPACE catalyzes discovery in heterogeneous tissues. SPACE datasets will fuel generative AI models for causal biology inference, accelerating drug discovery and precision medicine.
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:1495:3720,Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Predicting Cellular Responses to Gene Perturbation,retrievalaugmented generation for predicting cellular responses to gene perturbation,,,,2603.07233v1,"Predicting how cells respond to genetic perturbations is fundamental to understanding gene function, disease mechanisms, and therapeutic development. While recent deep learning approaches have shown promise in modeling single-cell perturbation responses, they struggle to generalize across cell types and perturbation contexts due to limited contextual information during generation. We introduce PT-RAG (Perturbation-aware Two-stage Retrieval-Augmented Generation), a novel framework that extends Retrieval-Augmented Generation beyond traditional language-model applications to cellular biology. Unlike standard RAG systems designed for text retrieval with pre-trained LLMs, perturbation retrieval lacks established similarity metrics and requires learning what constitutes relevant context, making differentiable retrieval essential. PT-RAG addresses this through a two-stage pipeline: first, retrieving candidate perturbations $K$ using GenePT embeddings, then adaptively refining the selection through Gumbel-Softmax discrete sampling conditioned on both the cell state and the input perturbation. This cell-type-aware differentiable retrieval enables end-to-end optimization of the retrieval objective jointly with generation. On the Replogle-Nadig single-gene perturbation dataset, we demonstrate that PT-RAG outperforms both STATE and vanilla RAG under identical experimental conditions, with the strongest gains in distributional similarity metrics ($W_1$, $W_2$). Notably, vanilla RAG's dramatic failure is itself a key finding: it demonstrates that differentiable, cell-type-aware retrieval is essential in this domain, and that naive retrieval can actively harm performance. Our results establish retrieval-augmented generation as a promising paradigm for modelling cellular responses to gene perturbation. The code to reproduce our experiments is available at https://github.com/difra100/PT-RAG_ICLR.",Andrea Giuseppe Di Francesco; Andrea Rubbi; Pietro Liò,2026.0,,2026-03-07,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,[],3,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,628,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework for predicting cellular responses, indicating a primary model paper with a biological modality. It contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework for predicting cellular responses, indicating a primary model paper with a biological modality. It contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""retrieval-augmented generation"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework that generates cellular response predictions, indicating a genuine generative architecture. It proposes a new model rather than a wrapper, fitting the primary model paper category.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,retrieval-augmented generation,clear_include,"The abstract presents PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework that generates cellular response predictions, indicating a genuine generative architecture. It proposes a new model rather than a wrapper, fitting the primary model paper category.",EXCLUDE,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|PASS,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,unclear,,,,,,,"The abstract introduces PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework for predicting cellular responses, indicating a primary model paper with a biological modality. It contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents PT‑RAG, a novel retrieval‑augmented generation framework that generates cellular response predictions, indicating a genuine generative architecture. It proposes a new model rather than a wrapper, fitting the primary model paper category.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:2491:4029,Decoding crop stress responses through multi-omics integration and computational modeling for climate-smart farming,decoding crop stress responses through multiomics integration and computational modeling for climatesmart farming,,,,,"The rapidly increasing effects of climate change are a serious threat to agricultural productivity and food security worldwide. To address this, the paradigm of crop stress biology is shifting due to the incorporation of multi-omics technologies and computational intelligence. This review highlights how holistic omics approaches, enhanced by artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML), are deciphering the intricate molecular networks governing plant stress responses. Such technologies are transforming the traditional breeding approach to crops into a predictive science, providing high-resolution insights into gene functions, stress-responsive pathways, and phenotypic traits. We discuss AI applications in dynamic crop monitoring, gene prioritisation, trait selection, and CRISPR-based genome editing, and highlight how they can be used to improve crop resilience and adaptability. In addition, climate-smart farming solutions are being developed in the wake of the emergence of digital agriculture, a product of big data and systems biology technologies. All these integrative tools, combined, can create opportunities for sustainable agriculture that have never existed before and ensure food production in a time of climatic uncertainty.","['MH Bilal', 'M Amir', 'QEA Qaseem', 'S Iqbal', 'M Waseem']",2026.0,Data Plus,,https://dataplus.org.pk/journal/index.php/live/article/view/73,['google_scholar'],1,[],1,,crossref_title,171.0,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,936,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract identifies the work as a review of multi‑omics and AI methods, not a primary model paper, and it contains no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio bridge.""}",review_editorial,yes,no,no,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract identifies the work as a review of multi‑omics and AI methods, not a primary model paper, and it contains no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a review of AI/ML methods for crop stress biology and does not mention any generative architecture such as decoder or autoregressive generation.""}",review_editorial,no,no,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract describes a review of AI/ML methods for crop stress biology and does not mention any generative architecture such as decoder or autoregressive generation.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,yes,no,no,no,no,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract identifies the work as a review of multi‑omics and AI methods, not a primary model paper, and it contains no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio bridge. | The abstract describes a review of AI/ML methods for crop stress biology and does not mention any generative architecture such as decoder or autoregressive generation.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:2934:                  The combination of pooled genetic perturbation screening with single-cell RNA-sequencing has allowed for large-scale profiling of single-cell transcriptional responses, providing valuable insights into gene function and cellular behavior. There is a need for computational methods capable of predicting single-cell responses to
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11561/guideline_pilot_results.csv:2958:4220,Departures: Distributional transport for single-cell perturbation prediction with neural schrödinger bridges,departures distributional transport for singlecell perturbation prediction with neural schrödinger bridges,,,,,"Predicting single-cell perturbation outcomes directly advances gene function analysis and facilitates drug candidate selection, making it a key driver of both basic and translational biomedical research. However, a major bottleneck in this task is the unpaired nature of single-cell data, as the same cell cannot be observed both before and after perturbation due to the destructive nature of sequencing. Although some neural generative transport models attempt to tackle unpaired single-cell perturbation data, they either lack explicit conditioning or depend on prior spaces for indirect distribution alignment, limiting precise perturbation modeling. In this work, we approximate Schrödinger Bridge (SB), which defines stochastic dynamic mappings recovering the entropy-regularized optimal transport (OT), to directly align the distributions of control and perturbed single-cell populations across different perturbation conditions. Unlike prior SB approximations that rely on bidirectional modeling to infer optimal source-target sample coupling, we leverage Minibatch-OT based pairing to avoid such bidirectional inference and the associated ill-posedness of defining the reverse process. This pairing directly guides bridge learning, yielding a scalable approximation to the SB. We approximate two SB models, one modeling discrete gene activation states and the other continuous expression distributions. Joint training enables accurate perturbation modeling and captures single-cell heterogeneity. Experiments on public genetic and drug perturbation datasets show that our model effectively captures heterogeneous single-cell responses and achieves state-of-the-art performance.","['C Chi', 'Y Huang', 'J Xia', 'J Zheng', 'Y Liu']",2026.0,Proceedings of the AAAI …,,https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/39190,['google_scholar'],1,[],1,,crossref_title,183.0,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,1127,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a new Schrödinger Bridge model for single‑cell perturbation prediction, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents a new Schrödinger Bridge model for single‑cell perturbation prediction, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work introduces a Schrödinger Bridge model for predicting single‑cell perturbation outcomes, without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism, and provides no indication of broad pretraining or transferability.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,none,"The work introduces a Schrödinger Bridge model for predicting single‑cell perturbation outcomes, without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism, and provides no indication of broad pretraining or transferability.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,no,,,,,,,"The abstract presents a new Schrödinger Bridge model for single‑cell perturbation prediction, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The work introduces a Schrödinger Bridge model for predicting single‑cell perturbation outcomes, without mentioning a decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism, and provides no indication of broad pretraining or transferability.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
