 by province and ministry. 720\n721\n722\n723\n724\n725\n726\n727\n728\n729\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAuthor contributions\nM.C. and  C.F. supervised  the  study.  Y .S.  designed  the study,  collected  data,  trained  the  model,  and performed further analysis with support from G.C. and J.W. Y .S. wrote the manuscript with input from G.C. and D.C. All authors reviewed and approved the submitted manuscript.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nCompeting interests:\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\navailable under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n730\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled `a` through `h`, apparently describing an RNA/tokenization/modeling workflow and dataset statistics.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel `a`: Schematic pipeline starting from an NCBI FASTA/GenBank source, genome/transcript sequence data, RNA-related categories, and a large vocabulary/tokenizer component. It appears to show biological sequence inputs being processed into a model-ready representation.\n- Panel `b`: Pie chart splitting a dataset or sequence collection into two categories, with visible proportions around `73.1%` and `26.9%`.\n- Panel `c`: Overlaid histogram/distribution plot of sequence length, with two colored groups and a long-tailed length distribution.\n- Panel `d`: Two pie charts showing category composition. The left chart includes a large green segment around `51.1%`; the right chart includes a green segment around `43.5%`. Labels are small but appear to represent biological RNA or sequence categories.\n- Panel `e`: Diagram of tokenization from a “Multimodal Input Sequence” into a “BPE Token Sequence” and then “Token IDs.” It explicitly shows nucleotide/RNA-like text input and a byte-pair encoding tokenizer mapping tokens to numeric IDs.\n- Panel `f`: Donut chart titled around “Vocabulary 6000,” showing token-type composition with a legend listing token types and counts.\n- Panel `g`: Bar chart showing coverage or percentage by token length/order, decreasing from the first token class to later classes.\n- Panel `h`: Scatter/line plot comparing token counts or similar values between two methods/sets; blue and red points/lines lie close to the diagonal, suggesting high agreement/correlation.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied:\n- RNA or nucleotide sequences.\n- FASTA/GenBank records.\n- RNA-related categories or structural/task labels.\n- Tokenized biological sequence vocabulary.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Biological sequence input is transformed through BPE tokenization.\n- Tokens are mapped to integer token IDs.\n- The resulting representation appears intended for a genomic/RNA foundation model or language model interface.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Dataset/category proportions are summarized by pie charts.\n- Sequence lengths are long-tailed.\n- Vocabulary composition is broken down by token types.\n- Token coverage decreases with token class/rank/length.\n- Token count comparison shows near-diagonal agreement between two tokenization/counting schemes.\n731\n732\n733\n734\n735\n736\n737\n738\n739\n740\n741\nFig. 1: Architecture design and multimodal pretraining corpus of the GenNA foundation model\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\na ,  Overview  of  the  GenNA  pretraining  pipeline,  illustrating  the  integration  of  multimodal  nucleotide sequences and natural-language annotations via a decoder-only Transformer. b , Proportion of genomic DNA and RNA in the pretraining corpus. c , Density distribution of sequence character lengths for DNA and  RNA. d ,  Taxonomic  distribution  of  the  pretraining  corpus  across  major  eukaryotic  lineages, measured by sequence count (left) and total character count (right). e ,  Schematic of the cross-modal BPE  tokenization  process  mapping  multimodal  sequence  and  text  inputs  to  unified  token  IDs. f , Composition  of  the  6,000-token  BPE  vocabulary  categorized  by  token  type  and  k-mer  length. g , Sequence  coverage  rates  achieved  by  different  k-mer  lengths  within  the  vocabulary.  h,  Correlation between  character  count  and  token  count,  demonstrating  sequence  compression  efficiency.  The  red dashed line represents the linear fit (Pearson's ).\n742\n743\n744\n745\n\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 2: GenNA internalizes sequence-function mappings and evolutionary constraints\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure labeled **a-j**. The figure appears to analyze **mRNA sequence regions**, **protein levels**, and **translation-related features** across cell or sample types.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Heatmap comparing **protein levels** between “initial one-symbol” and “predicted one-symbol” groupings. Rows and columns are labeled with sequence-like or codon-related categories. Color scale runs from dark purple/blue to yellow, indicating low to high values.\n- **Panel b:** Boxplot comparing **waterfall** and **microarray** measurements, with y-axis labeled as an mRNA log ratio or expression-related quantity.\n- **Panel c:** Triangular contact/interaction-style heatmap over an **mRNA sequence**, annotated with regions including **5' UTR**, **CDS**, and **3' UTR**. A teal rectangle marks the **CDS** region. Color bar indicates interaction or similarity strength.\n- **Panel d:** Boxplots showing **attention ratio** across gene regions: **5' UTR**, **CDS**, and **3' UTR**. Legend lists attention-on categories such as **prefix**, **tag**, **5' UTR**, **CDS**, and **3' UTR**.\n- **Panel e:** Horizontal bar/boxplot-style feature contribution plot labeled with **delta protein** or protein change relative to wild type. Features include **CDS distance**, **CDS skewness**, **CDS stop loss**, and UTR-related terms.\n- **Panel f:** Two heatmaps aligned to sequence position. Left shows attention or contribution across full mRNA regions labeled **5' UTR**, **CDS (coding sequence)**, and **3' UTR**. Right zooms near a **start codon**, marked by a red vertical box and text “Start Codon.”\n- **Panel g:** Two t-SNE scatterplots. Left separates **medication type** or class into two color groups. Right shows clusters colored by **language/species-like labels** or categories, with a legend of multiple groups.\n- **Panel h:** Horizontal bar chart of frequency values for biological or functional categories; top bar is highlighted red, remaining bars blue/gray.\n- **Panel i:** Heatmap comparing **targeted small molecules** or perturbation classes against **human tissue/cell-type** categories, with color scale indicating protein change or fold change.\n- **Panel j:** Two phylogenetic/tree diagrams comparing **translated mRNA/RNA length** and **tree based on gene/TSS or codon-type metric**, with branches colored green and orange.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nBiological source objects visible or implied: **mRNA sequences**, **5' UTR**, **coding sequence/CDS**, **3' UTR**, **start codon**, protein expression/protein levels, sequence-derived features, tissue or cell-type categories, and species/organism-like groupings.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible: heatmaps, attention-ratio summaries, feature-contribution plots, sequence-position attribution maps, dimensionality reduction using **t-SNE**, boxplots, bar charts, and phylogenetic/tree comparisons.\n\nLikely findings shown visually: sequence regions, especially the **CDS** and start-codon-proximal positions, appear to contribute strongly to predicted protein-level changes; attention/contribution differs by transcript region; sequence-derived features and biological groupings separate samples in heatmaps and t-SNE space.\na , Perplexity (PPL) heatmap evaluating the alignment between real nucleotide sequences and provided gene anntotation prompts ( genes). Warmer colors indicate higher PPL. b , Perplexity for matched available under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint versus  mismatched  sequence-function  pairs. c ,  Token-to-token  self-attention  map  extracted  from 746 Transformer Layer 18 during autoregressive sequence generation. d , Dynamic attention ratio allocated to 747 prompt tags versus upstream sequence components during the generation of the 5 ′ UTR, CDS, and 3 ′ 748 UTR ( g1866 g3404  100 sequences). e , Zero-shot in silico variant effect prediction across different mutation types 749 in  protein-coding  genes  ( g1866  g3404  100 sequences). Δ PPL represents the difference in perplexity between 750 mutant  and  wild-type  sequences. f ,  High-resolution Δ PPL  landscape  along  a  representative  coding 751 sequence. g ,  t-SNE  visualization  of  intermediate-layer  sequence  embeddings,  colored  by  taxonomic 752 group  (left) and  molecule  type  (right). h ,  Baseline  perplexity  for  native  sequences  across  10 753 representative species. i , Δ PPL matrix induced by species prompt swapping. j , Topological comparison 754 between the hierarchical clustering tree derived from GenNA's pairwise Δ PPL (left) and the biological 755 reference phylogenetic tree (right). 756\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about biological sequence generation and evaluation.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- Panel **a**: Workflow schematic with three rows labeled **Unconditional Generation**, **Species-specific Generation**, and **Downstream Task Generation**. Columns are **Prompt**, **Generated Metadata**, and **Generated Sequence**. Prompts include examples such as RNA, Homo sapiens, and histone H1 with special tokens. Generated outputs show metadata text followed by RNA/protein-like generated sequences with colored highlighted segments and special tokens.\n\n- Panels **b** and **c**: Scatter plots comparing semantic similarity against sequence quality scores. Points are colored in a viridis-like gradient, with marginal histograms on top and right. Panel b reports a **Spearman ρ ≈ 0.32** and **macro similarity ≈ 0.72**. Panel c reports **Spearman ρ ≈ 0.43** and **mean similarity ≈ 0.70**. X axes appear to be model/sequence-quality scores; y axes are semantic similarity.\n\n- Panel **d**: Density plots comparing **Real Genomes** and **Generated Sequences**. Two subplots show distributions for **CDS GC3 content (%)** and **CDS GC content (%)**. Generated sequences are shown in orange and real genomes in dark blue/gray, with generated distributions shifted higher.\n\n- Panel **e**: Boxplots comparing **generated** versus **real genome** values across organisms: **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. Metrics shown include **CDS U/C content (%)** and **CDS GC3 content (%)**. Yellow star markers indicate real-genome values overlaid on generated distributions.\n\n- Panel **f**: Circular/radar-style plots for the same organisms: **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. Each compares **Real** versus **Generated** profiles using organism-specific colors.\n\n- Panel **g**: Bar charts comparing amino-acid frequencies between **generated** and **real genome** sequences for **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. X axes list amino-acid one-letter codes; y axes are frequency.\n\nBiological source objects visible: RNA sequences, coding sequences/CDS, genomes, protein or amino-acid sequences, and species-specific genomes from human, zebrafish, fruit fly, Arabidopsis, yeast, and maize.\n\nOverall visible finding: generated biological sequences are evaluated against real genomes using semantic similarity, sequence-quality scores, GC/GC3 composition, codon or nucleotide-content distributions, circular composition profiles, and amino-acid frequency comparisons.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\navailable under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\na ,  Schematic  of  the  three  different  generation paradigms:  unconditional  self-guided  generation, 759 species-specific  generation,  and  downstream  task-directed  generation. b , c ,  Cross-modal  semantic 760 consistency of generated sequences from unconditional and species-specific generation tasks. Scatter 761 plots  show  cosine  similarity  between  generated  text  metadata  and  BLASTP-derived  functional 762 annotations, with horizontal dashed lines indicating the high-confidence threshold (0.8). Marginal plots 763 show data density. d ,  Probability density distributions of CDS GC content (top) and CDS GC3 content 764 (bottom)  for  sequences  generated  by  GenNA  versus  real  gene  sequences  of  fruit  fly (Drosophila 765 melanogaster) . e , Comparison  of CDS  GC  (top)  and  CDS  GC3  (bottom)  content  across  six 766 representative eukaryotic model organisms. f , Radar charts comparing synonymous codon usage bias 767 between real reference sequences (dashed black lines) and generated sequences (solid colored lines). g , 768 Global amino acid frequencies of real and generated sequences across 6 model organisms. 769\n770\n771\n772\n773\n774\n775\n776\n777\n\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nFig. 4: Controllable generation of structured non-coding RNAs and protein-coding sequences\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-h**, showing analyses of generated biological macromolecular/RNA sequence or structure outputs compared with natural/reference data.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Scatter/strip plot of **gRNA-miRNA-seq score**, grouped by categories including **Exact match**, **wobble pairing**, **isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and likely **No detection**. Horizontal reference lines are shown. Points are color-coded by category.\n- **Panel b:** Stacked bar chart showing **percentage (%)** outcomes across amino-acid/tRNA-like labels. Legend categories include **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and **No detection**.\n- **Panel c:** Histogram/density plot of **sequence length (%)**, comparing **Class II (all tRNAs)** vs **Class I (tRNAs)** or similar class labels.\n- **Panel d:** Violin plots comparing **Real** vs **Generated** distributions for internal bit scores across RNA families: **5S**, **5.8S**, **16S**, and **28S**.\n- **Panel e:** Multiple scatter plots of **Isoelectric Point (pI)** vs **Molecular Weight (kDa)** for **natural sequences** and generated sequences under different **temperature / Top-p** sampling settings. Points are colored by **histone type**, with labels such as **H1**, **H2A**, **H2B**, **H3**, and **H4**.\n- **Panel f:** Boxplots of **ProGen2 perplexity** grouped by **histone type**, comparing natural sequences and generated outputs under several sampling conditions.\n- **Panel g:** Predicted protein/disordered-region visualization with labeled **N-term**, **C-term**, **Intrinsic Disordered Regions**, and **Globular Domain**. A confidence color scale labeled **pLDDT confidence** ranges roughly from low red/orange to high blue.\n- **Panel h:** Four circular/radial structural or sequence maps comparing **Natural Sequence** and **Generated** traces under different temperature/top-p sampling settings.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied: **tRNAs or tRNA-like sequences**, **ribosomal RNA families 5S/5.8S/16S/28S**, and **histone proteins H1/H2A/H2B/H3/H4**.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible: comparison of **real/natural vs generated sequences**, generated under different **sampling parameters** such as **temperature** and **Top-p**.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nLikely findings shown visually: generated sequences are being evaluated against natural biological sequences by matching class, sequence length, covariance/internal bit score distributions, protein physicochemical properties, perplexity, predicted structure confidence, disorder regions, and circular structural/sequence representations.\na ,  Distribution  of  tRNAscan-SE  Cove  scores  for  generated  tRNAs,  categorized  by  anticodon  pairing behavior.  Solid  horizontal  lines  represent  medians. b ,  Proportion  of  anticodon  pairing  strategies spontaneously  adopted  by the  model  when  generating tRNAs for specific  amino  acids. c ,  Sequence length distribution for generated Class II (Leu/Ser) and Class I (others) tRNAs. d , Infernal bit scores for natural and generated ribosomal RNAs (5S, 5.8S, 18S, 28S). e , Physicochemical landscape (isoelectric available under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint point and molecular weight) of natural core histones and GenNA-generated histones across 778 incrementally increasing sampling temperatures and top-p thresholds. f , ProGen2 perplexity scores for 779 natural and generated sequences across five histone types. g , Schematic of the 3D spatial domains of a 780 histone, highlighting intrinsic disordered regions (IDRs). The structure is colored by AlphaFold2 pLDDT 781 confidence score (blue indicates high confidence; warm colors indicate low confidence). h , Radar charts 782 showing amino acid composition preferences of natural and generated histones modulated by different 783 sampling parameters. 784\n785\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a–h** describing a multimodal nucleotide sequence foundation model and tokenizer analysis.\n\nPanel **a** shows a workflow for **multi-modal nucleotide sequences**. Inputs include **NCBI RefSeq GenBank files** with plasmids/genomes/chromosomes, plus hybrid nucleotide language materials: **genomic DNA and RNA**, **natural language annotations**, and **XML structural tags**. These are processed by a **cross-modal BPE tokenizer** with **6,000 vocabulary size** and used to train a **GenNA Foundation Model**, described as a **Qwen3 decoder-only transformer with 3.6B parameters**. The model interface is shown for causal language modeling / next-token prediction over mixed biological and annotation tokens. Output capabilities include sequence-function alignment, understanding biological rules, zero-shot variant effect prediction, species-specific genomic fingerprints, latent-space clustering, phylogenetic tree reconstruction, unconditional self-guided generation, targeted ncRNA generation, and protein-coding sequence generation.\n\nPanel **b** is a pie chart showing source composition: **RNA 73.1%** and **genomic DNA 26.9%**.\n\nPanel **c** is a length distribution histogram comparing **genomic DNA** and **RNA** sequence lengths in characters. Both distributions are concentrated at shorter lengths with a long tail extending toward 20,000 characters.\n\nPanel **d** contains two taxonomic composition pie charts. Visible groups include **plant**, **invertebrate**, **vertebrate mammalian**, **vertebrate other**, **fungi**, and **protozoa**, with vertebrate other and plant comprising large portions.\n\nPanel **e** illustrates cross-modal BPE tokenization of a multimodal input sequence containing a tRNA annotation and nucleotide sequence, e.g. `...tRNA-Glu<seq>ATAC<gene><tRNA>TATTT...`. The sequence is transformed into a **BPE token sequence**, then mapped into **token IDs**.\n\nPanel **f** is a donut chart of tokenizer vocabulary composition for **vocabulary 6000**, broken down by token type and count. It includes nucleotide k-mers from **1-mer** through **9-mer and above**, plus **N-containing kmers** and **non-nucleotide** tokens. The largest visible segment is **7-mer**, followed by **8-mer** and **6-mer** categories.\n\nPanel **g** is a bar chart of **coverage by k-mer length**, showing decreasing coverage as k-mer length increases. Bars are labeled for k=1 through k=8 with percentages, including **100.0%** for 1-mers and lower coverage for longer k-mers.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nPanel **h** is a scatter plot comparing **character count** and **token count**, with a fitted line. The relationship is highly linear, with visible annotation approximately **Fit: y = 0.199x** and **r = 0.999**.\navailable under a CC-BY 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.22.720063; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about a genomics or sequence model evaluated on gene symbols, nucleotide regions, mutations, molecule types, species, and phylogenetic structure.\n\nVisible panels:\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\n- **a**: Heatmap comparing “Real Gene Symbol” rows against “Provided Gene Symbol (Prompt)” columns. Gene symbols include ALKBH7, CPNE1, LYRM9, NAA16, PEX10, PNILPR1, RXFP3, SPSB3, TFAP2B. Colorbar is “Perplexity (Log Scale).” Diagonal entries are low/dark, off-diagonal entries are higher/yellow-green, indicating lower perplexity when the prompt gene symbol matches the real gene.\n- **b**: Boxplot of perplexity on log scale comparing “Matched” vs “Mismatched.” Mismatched prompts have much higher perplexity than matched prompts.\n- **c**: Triangular attention matrix labeled by token index query/key. Regions are annotated as 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR, with an “Attention Weight” colorbar. Shows attention structure across sequence regions.\n- **d**: Boxplots of “Attention Ratio” by current generating region: 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Legend “Attention On” includes Prompt, Tag, 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Indicates attention is concentrated on different context regions depending on the region being generated.\n- **e**: Horizontal boxplots of Δ Perplexity (Mut - WT) for mutation classes: CDS Deletion, CDS Nonsense, CDS Stop-Loss, CDS Missense, CDS Synonymous, 3' UTR Substitution, 5' UTR Substitution, 3' UTR Deletion, 5' UTR Deletion. Coding-disruptive mutations show larger positive Δ perplexity.\n- **f**: Two mutation-position heatmaps. Rows are mutated bases A, C, G, T, and DEL. Columns are nucleotide positions. Top annotations mark 5' UTR, CDS, and 3' UTR. Colorbar is Δ Perplexity (Mut - WT). Deletions in CDS show strong positive effects; a zoomed heatmap highlights the start codon region.\n- **g**: Two t-SNE scatterplots. Left colored by molecule type, DNA vs RNA. Right colored by species groups: fungi, invertebrate, plant, protozoa, vertebrate mammalian, vertebrate other. Shows clustering by molecule type and taxonomic grouping.\n- **h**: Horizontal bar chart of perplexity by species/source object. Includes Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Arabidopsis thaliana, Drosophila melanogaster, Homo sapiens, and other species. Values vary substantially by species.\n- **i**: Heatmap of Δ Perplexity / penalty for true species label versus provided species label prompt. Species include Homo sapiens, Mus musculus, Gallus gallus, Danio rerio, Drosophila melanogaster, Caenorhabditis elegans, Arabidopsis thaliana, Oryza sativa Japonica Group, Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, and Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\n- **j**: Two phylogenetic/tree diagrams: “Tree based on PPL Penalty” and “Tree based on Ground Truth.” Compares model-derived sequence-distance structure with biological taxonomy.\nBiological source objects: nucleotide sequences with annotated 5' UTR, CDS/coding sequence, and 3' UTR regions; gene symbols; mutation classes; species labels across animals, plants, fungi, and other eukaryotes.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: prompted gene-symbol and species-label conditioning, perplexity measurement, mutation perturbation Δ perplexity, attention maps/attention ratios, t-SNE embeddings, and tree reconstruction from perplexity penalty.\n\nMain visible findings: matched biological prompts reduce perplexity; coding-region disruptive mutations increase perplexity most; attention differs by generated sequence region; learned representations cluster by molecule type/species; perplexity penalties recover some taxonomic structure.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological sequence generation figure.\n\nPanel **a**: Schematic workflow with three rows:\n- **Unconditional Generation**: prompt box `RNA,` leads to generated metadata such as “RNA, entelurus aequoreus, tripartite motif-containing protein 16 <seq>”, then to generated sequence containing tokens like `<seq><gene>` and nucleotide text.\n- **Species-specific Generation**: prompt `RNA, Homo sapiens,` leads to generated human metadata for `cdc42bpa, serine/threonine-protein kinase mrck alpha isoform 1 <seq>`, then generated sequence.\n- **Downstream Task Generation**: prompt `RNA, histone H1<seq>` directly leads to generated sequence with `<gene>`, `<cds>`, and nucleotide sequence tokens.\n\nPanels **b** and **c**: Scatterplots comparing generated sequences by:\n- x-axis: **BLAST Bit Score (Sequence Quality)**\n- y-axis: **Semantic Similarity**\n- marginal histograms on top and right\n- colored point clouds with trend curves\n- annotations:\n  - panel b: Spearman rho = 0.32, mean similarity = 0.79\n  - panel c: Spearman rho = 0.43, mean similarity = 0.70\n\nPanel **d**: Density plots comparing **Real Genome** and **Generated Sequences** distributions:\n- top: **CDS GC Content (%)**\n- bottom: **CDS GC3 Content (%)**\nGenerated sequences appear shifted toward higher GC and GC3 content than real genome distributions.\n\nPanel **e**: Boxplots comparing generated CDS nucleotide composition across species, with star markers for real genome values:\n- species: Human, Zebrafish, Fruit Fly, Arabidopsis, Yeast, Maize\n- top y-axis: **CDS GC Content (%)**\n- bottom y-axis: **CDS GC3 Content (%)**\n\nPanel **f**: Polar/radar-style codon usage plots for:\n- Human\n- Zebrafish\n- Fruit Fly\n- Arabidopsis\n- Yeast\n- Maize  \nEach compares real genome codon patterns against generated sequence patterns.\n\nPanel **g**: Amino acid frequency bar charts for the same six species:\n- Human, Zebrafish, Fruit Fly, Arabidopsis, Yeast, Maize\n- x-axis lists amino acid one-letter codes\n- y-axis: **Frequency**\n- legend compares **Generated** vs **Real Genome**\n\nVisible biological source objects include RNA sequences, coding sequences, genes, codons, amino acid frequencies, GC/GC3 content, and species-specific genomes from human, zebrafish, fruit fly, Arabidopsis, yeast, and maize.\n\nThe figure appears to evaluate a generative biological sequence model by comparing generated RNA/CDS outputs with real genomes using BLAST sequence quality, semantic similarity, nucleotide composition, codon usage, and amino acid frequency. A bioRxiv preprint watermark overlays the middle of the image.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure with panels labeled **a-h**.\n\nPanel descriptions:\n- **a**: Violin/scatter distributions of **NHAlignES score (bits)** for tRNA-related categories: **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, and **Incorrect isotype**. A horizontal reference line is shown around 70 bits.\n- **b**: Stacked bar chart by amino acid/isotype labels from **Gln** through **Phe**, showing percentages of categories: **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and **No detection**.\n- **c**: Histogram/density plot of **sequence length (nt)** comparing **Class II (Leu/Ser)** and **Class I (Others)**.\n- **d**: Violin plots comparing **Real** vs **Generated** sequences for internal bit scores across rRNA-like groups labeled **5S**, **5.8S**, **18S**, and **28S**.\n- **e**: Scatter plots of protein **Isoelectric Point (pI)** versus **Molecular Weight (kDa)**. Columns compare **Natural Sequences** and generated sequence settings: **Temperature 0.55 Top-p 0.7**, **Temperature 0.7 Top-p 0.8**, **Temperature 0.85 Top-p 0.9**, and **Temperature 1.0 Top-p 0.95**. Points are colored by histone type: **H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4**.\n- **f**: Boxplots of **ProtGen2 Perplexity** across histone types **H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4**, comparing natural sequences and generated sequences under the same temperature/top-p settings.\n- **g**: Protein structure visualization colored by **pLDDT confidence** scale from 40 to 100. Labels identify **N-term**, **C-term**, **Intrinsic Disordered Regions**, and **Globular Domain**.\n- **h**: Four polar/radar-style codon-frequency or codon-distribution plots comparing **Natural Sequence** with generated sequences under different temperature/top-p settings.\n\nBiological source objects visible:\n- tRNA/isotype categories and amino-acid labels.\n- rRNA categories: 5S, 5.8S, 18S, 28S.\n- Histone protein classes: H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4.\n- Protein structural regions including disordered termini and globular domain.\n\nGenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\nAdditional information\nFig. 3: Large-scale unconditional and species-specific sequence generation 758\nModel/interface elements:\n- Generated sequence settings controlled by **temperature** and **top-p** sampling.\n- Evaluation metrics include alignment/internal bit scores, sequence length, molecular weight, isoelectric point, ProtGen2 perplexity, pLDDT confidence, and codon distribution.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Exact matches generally show high NHAlignES scores compared with incorrect isotypes.\n- Generated rRNA-like sequences are compared directly against real sequences by internal bit score.\n- Generated histone-like sequences shift in molecular-weight/pI space as temperature/top-p increase.\n- Higher temperature/top-p settings appear to broaden generated sequence distributions.\n- Protein structure panel shows a compact globular domain with long low-confidence/disordered terminal regions.\n=== END ===\n\n=== NODE PATH:  (TaxonomyCodedDocument) ===\nInstance identifiers to fill (1 instances):\n[\n  {\n    \"title\": \"GenNA: Conditional generation of nucleotide sequences guided by natural-language annotations\"\n  }\n]\n\n=== SCHEMA (fill all fields per instance) ===\n{\n  \"$defs\": {\n    \"TaxonomyCodedRoute\": {\n      \"description\": \"One route coded against taxonomy v1 while retaining lossless evidence.\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"route_label\": {\n          \"title\": \"Route Label\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"model_name\": {\n          \"title\": \"Model Name\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"lifecycle_phase\": {\n          \"enum\": [\n            \"pretraining\",\n            \"fine_tuning\",\n            \"inference\",\n            \"evaluation\",\n            \"unclear\"\n          ],\n          \"title\": \"Lifecycle Phase\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": {\n          \"title\": \"Task Or Configuration Verbatim\",\n      